<p>In the latest turn in the US trade dispute, President Trump said he is raising an across-the-board “worldwide” tariff on imports into the United States from 10% to 15%. The increase follows a US Supreme Court decision that largely struck down his earlier, broader tariff program, which relied on emergency powers under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA). The administration, echoed by multiple outlets, says the new tariff rests on a different legal foundation: Section 122 of the Trade Act of 1974. Politico also reported that, when Trump announced the 15% level, the White House had not yet officially put the increase into effect. For global partners and businesses, the immediate takeaway is fresh uncertainty: how durable US tariff policy will be, which exemptions apply alongside overlapping levies, and whether companies will seek repayments for duties collected under measures the court has now invalidated.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/yTzzpg78ggv9hNmZRZ7X6YxkjOkeNYNGCWYGpPCSxbC490pQC1d-TG0QG3N3bvtx_uQCWEoI8C0TWE_7OjyZvjyf8KSV20CeNmWo2G1pVuDC' alt='US President Donald Trump speaks at the White House following the Supreme Court ruling on tariffs in Washington, DC, February 20, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Capped authority: Section 122 is time-limited and capped at 15%, so Trump’s move to 15% essentially maxes out what this statute allows unless Congress acts or the administration turns to another legal tool.</li><li>Implementation uncertainty: Politico reported the White House had not officially implemented the higher rate at the time of Trump’s announcement, and other reporting says the shift would require updates to the prior day’s proclamation and related measures.</li><li>Stacking and carve-outs: Reporting says the new across-the-board tariff stacks on top of existing levies, while exemptions may apply for certain sectors or goods covered by existing trade agreements.</li><li>Refund questions: Businesses are seeking repayment for an estimated $133bn already collected under earlier tariffs, as reporting raises the prospect of refunds after the Supreme Court ruling.</li></ul><blockquote>Let this statement serve to represent that I, as president of the United States of America, will be, effective immediately, raising the 10% Worldwide Tariff on Countries … to the fully allowed, and legally tested, 15% level - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: He called the Supreme Court decision “extraordinarily anti-American” and said the administration would pursue “new and legally permissible” tariffs in the coming months. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974820/effective-immediately-trump-announces-hike-in-us-global-tariff-rate-from-10pc-to-15pc'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>White House implementation status: Politico reported that the White House had not officially implemented the higher tariffs at the time of Trump’s statement announcing the 15% level. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/02/21/trump-tariff-supreme-court-00792288'>Politico</a>)</li><li>India: India said it is “studying developments” and reviewing the potential impact on trade after the Supreme Court ruling. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/studying-developments-india-says-reviewing-impact-of-us-supreme-court-ruling-against-trump-tariffs-13982296.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/trump-raises-global-tariffs-from-10-to-15-day-after-big-supreme-court-ruling-11118639#publisher=newsstand'>Trump Hikes Global Tariffs From 10% To 15% Day After Big Supreme Court Order</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/632880-trump-raise-global-tariff-court/'>Trump raises global tariff to 15%</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974820/effective-immediately-trump-announces-hike-in-us-global-tariff-rate-from-10pc-to-15pc'>‘Effective immediately’: Trump announces hike in US global tariff rate from 